a hard wall-clock cap of 3 hours for this session; plan the work so that the report section and PROGRESS.md are written well before that. If the full study would not fit, sample and say so in the report.  Read these two files first, in full:  --- TASK.md --- # TASK: Machine learning to understand the nature of MOSP — phase 4, the seven questions  ## Philosophy - Read `reports/ml_nature_plan_3.md` in full first: it is the plan this loop   executes and §0 lists what the three earlier loops established. Then read   the sections of `reports/ml_nature.md` your item cites, the one-page §0 of   `reports/ml_nature_summary.md`, and `CLAUDE.md`'s blocks \"What the corpus   says about the problem itself\", \"The generated-ensemble campaign\" and \"The   objects loop\". `reports/learning_plan.md` §4 and `reports/ml_nature_summary.md`   §9 list what is closed and must not be rebuilt. - **A prediction is never a bound.** Nothing learned may touch   `satisfiability/mosp_solver.py::_lower_bound` or any path that decides `k`.   A candidate bound is checked on every certified instance, attacked by the   extremal search, written up as a conjecture, and never used by the solver. - **No solver default changes.** Fix variants (item 04), new heuristics   (items 01, 02) and any flag are measured behind options that default to   today's behaviour, with paired node counts, and *proposed* in the report.   A new heuristic strategy may be registered in `satisfiability/heuristics.py`   as a named option; it may not become the default of anything. - **Every split groups by file ∪ MOSP-graph isomorphism class**   (`learning.fingerprint.union_groups`). - **Nodes, not seconds**; seconds beside them; a censored call is a lower   bound, never a missing value; SAT cost is counted in conflicts. - **State the size range every conclusion covers.** Corpus 6,376 at 9–134;   campaign 37,800 at 10–40, 6,747 at 50–75, a 5-per-cell sample at 100; item   07 adds one certified cell at 100. Nothing below 100 is evidence about   125 × 125 except through a stated extrapolation. - Every number in a report is regenerated by a command named in that report. - Findings that rest on a handful of instances are re-certified for those   instances (`solve_mosp_exact`, never `solutions_dir=None`) before being written.  - **Compute**: check `pgrep -f benchmarks.recertify`; if it is running, its   cores are not yours. 32 cores total; use up to 16 here. Each iteration has   a hard 3-hour wall-clock cap enforced by the driver; items 06 and 07 price   their runs before starting (`learning.cost_model` for 07) and sample if the   full run will not fit, saying so.  ## Current Focus `reports/ml_nature_plan_3.md` §1: thirteen items in the order written. Items 01–03 are cheap closures; 04–06 narrow soundness above 40 customers; 07–08 extend the ridge to 100; 09–10 explain the ridge; 11 and 12 equip the two proof problems; 13 is the reserve.  ## Target Problems (in order) See `iterations.md`. Work on exactly ONE unchecked item per iteration.  ## Acceptance Criteria (per item) - [ ] Code lives under `learning/` (or `lean/` for item 12, `satisfiability/`       for a registered heuristic or a flag) with a module docstring saying what       question it answers and how to run it; runnable as `python -m learning.<name>`. - [ ] A section appended to `reports/ml_nature.md` (continuing the numbering,       §28 onwards) with: the question, the method, the baseline, the table(s),       the finding in one paragraph, the size range covered, the kill       criterion's verdict where the plan states one, and the regenerate command. - [ ] `python -m pytest tests/ -q -x` passes (~85 s). New code that computes a       quantity gets at least one test on a tiny hand-checkable instance; a new       flag on the search gets a test that the default path is byte-for-byte       unchanged and a test that the flag never changes a `decide` status. - [ ] The item is marked `- [x]` (done) or `- [!]` (blocked, with a reason in       PROGRESS.md) in `iterations.md`. - [ ] No regressions, no changes to solver defaults, nothing written to       `solutions/`. Generated instances and witnesses go under       `learning/data/ensemble/` (committed; total under ~150 MB apparent).  ## Completion Conditions An item is DONE when either: 1. **Solved**: meets all criteria and the report section is written, OR 2. **Blocked**: documented in PROGRESS.md with the specific blocker. Items 05,    11 and 12 may end with a precise statement of what cannot be done and why;    that is a deliverable, not a failure.  ## Context - **Key files**: `learning/differential.py` (the harness; `--stage drawn`),   `learning/graph_story.py` (relabel, recover), `learning/relabel_portfolio.py`,   `learning/cost_model.py` (pricing for item 07), `learning/ensemble.py` and   `learning/upward.py` (the campaign; resumable), `learning/scale_test.py`   (`fit_scaling`, `scale_nodes.csv`), `learning/hardness_map.py`,   `learning/ridge_theory.py` (§25's candidates and peaks), `learning/extremal.py`   and `learning/treewidth.py` (the adversary and exact treewidth),   `learning/conjecture.py` (the validity oracle over 50,949 instances),   `learning/distil.py` (the two-key rule, `HYPOTHESES`), `learning/corpus_sweep.py`,   `satisfiability/customer_search.py` and `.c` (the search; `dominance_filter`   in the C is where the fix lives; `reports/better_move_bug.md` §7),   `satisfiability/heuristics.py` (`STRATEGIES`, `least_cost_node`, `restricted_dfs`),   `satisfiability/mosp_solver.py` (`decide_mosp`, the SAT path; `SAT_BACKEND`),   `satisfiability/mosp_encoding.py`, `learning/proofs.py` (DRAT),   `learning/degeneracy.py` (the lattice oracle), `lean/MOSPFormalization/Reduction.lean`   and `Sandwich.lean`, `literature/becceneri_yanasse_soma_2004_method_mosp_cutting.pdf`   (MCNh pseudocode §4, worked example Table 1 and Fig. 2),   `literature/plosone_2018_pagerank_mosp.pdf` (Frinhani's MCNh column),   `recertify/results.json` (the 125 × 125 counts;